Author: ed3c <ed3c@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Tue Sep 1 20:11:33 2026 +0800

    reconcile(arrival): name the arrival axis, derive the ledger, own one tree digest
    
    Three monitor findings against this branch, each fixed in the bytes rather
    than answered in prose.
    
    Arrival levels are DECLARED / EXERCISED / PRODUCTION, not L0 / L1 / L2. The
    branch as pushed shipped a validator clause requiring SKILL.md to disambiguate
    its levels from the two other axes in this repository that also count from L0.
    That is a gate coupled to prose, and it cures the collision rather than its
    cause. This axis was the newest of the three and had zero consumers, so it is
    the one that moves; the disambiguation paragraph and the gate that read it are
    both deleted.
    
    The arrival column is now derived, not remembered. `audit_row` compared with
    `>`, so an over-claim was a finding and an under-claim was invisible -- a row
    whose receipts had outgrown it stayed understated until an author remembered,
    which is exactly the stale list this ledger exists to kill. The comparison is
    equality, `CLAIM_BELOW_ARRIVAL` joins the diagnostic table with its own planted
    fixture row, and both readers now call one `arrival_mismatch()` instead of the
    validator carrying a second copy of the comparison.
    
    `audit_islands.tree_digest` is renamed `tree_fingerprint`. It never was this
    repository's tree identity -- `scripts/common.tree_digest` is, and the two
    selected different files over the same tree (`.git` kept vs skipped, symlinks
    raising vs tolerated), so one name covered two numbers. Importing the shared
    helper is the wrong fix here: the audited tree is usually another repository,
    where `.git` mutates on read and a symlink must not abort the pass.
    
    Conformance with the dual-standard sweep landed by #74: `SKILL_MD_CLAUSES`
    declared in the validator, a `skills/README.md` index row, and this bundle's
    permanent `SKILL_CHECKS` row. The admission receipt is still structurally
    absent and is stamped, never hand-authored, once #72's bootstrap entry for this
    tree lands.
    
    Refs ed3c/skill-concerns#73

